        "bodyTr": "Lee Hwa-young, former vice governor for peace of Gyeonggi Province, who is serving the fifth year of a confirmed seven-year-eight-month prison sentence in the \"Ssangbangwool illegal North Korea remittance\" case, is reportedly planning to issue a second statement from prison and demand early release including parole and special pardon. Earlier on the 7th, Kim Gwang-min, the attorney representing the former vice governor, posted on social media saying, \"The former vice governor is very angry and has said many times that he feels wronged by President Lee,\" demanding parole for the former vice governor. It is an unprecedented and awkward sight for a convicted criminal serving time with a confirmed sentence to demand release from prison. This has sparked speculation that there may be some unknown circumstances behind the scenes. The former vice governor and President Lee Jae-myung were both indicted as co-conspirators in the North Korea remittance case, in which 8 million dollars in expenses for a North Korea visit during his tenure as Gyeonggi governor were sent to North Korea through the Ssangbangwool Group. The former vice governor had stated that he reported the status of the North Korea remittance and visit to then-Governor Lee, but later retracted this statement and raised suspicions of \"statement manipulation for a salmon sushi party,\" which became the basis for claims that charges against President Lee should be dropped. With this at the forefront, the Democratic Party has been pursuing a special investigation into alleged prosecution manipulation following a national audit in April. Because of this, the sudden appearance of the former vice governor's unreasonable demand has sparked widespread talk that he may be attempting to negotiate with the president, implying that his revelations and additional statements could play a critical role in the president's legal risks. President Lee has consistently denied involvement, calling the North Korea remittance \"an unprecedented manipulation case.\" What is the truth after all? For the former vice governor, who received a confirmed guilty verdict from the Supreme Court, to attempt to bargain his release with the sitting president without any legitimacy or grounds is an irresponsible act that undermines the foundation of the rule of law. If the former vice governor truly feels wronged, or if there are matters he has failed to disclose, the priority should be to reveal all relevant facts truthfully concerning the truth of the North Korea remittance case, the retraction of statements made during trial proceedings, and the suspicions raised about the sushi party. If that is not the case, and there is nothing more to disclose beyond what was stated in court, he should not continue to issue statements from prison that create trouble out of nothing and pressure the president.",
